  "description": "<div>\n<p>Calculate x raised to the integer exponent n and return the numeric result. Negative n means take the reciprocal of x raised to its positive magnitude. Avoid multiplying |n| times when exponentiation by squaring can reduce the work.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 1:</strong></p>\n<pre><strong>Input:</strong> x = 2, n = 10\n<strong>Output:</strong> 1024\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Ten factors of 2 produce 1024.</pre>\n<p><strong>Example 2:</strong></p>\n<pre><strong>Input:</strong> x = 2, n = -2\n<strong>Output:</strong> 0.25\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> A negative exponent gives 1 divided by 2 squared.</pre>\n<p><strong>Example 3:</strong></p>\n<pre><strong>Input:</strong> x = -2, n = 3\n<strong>Output:</strong> -8\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> An odd exponent preserves the negative sign.</pre>\n<p><strong>Constraints:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>-2 &lt;= x &lt;= 2</code> and x is a finite number.</li>\n<li><code>-100 &lt;= n &lt;= 100</code>.</li>\n<li>If <code>x = 0</code>, then <code>n &gt; 0</code>.</li>\n<li>The result is a finite number.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>",
